    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d652">

                <pb facs="0703" n="695" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg695"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860C.001 Moscicki, Ignacy/28: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gunther</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-10-04T20:00:00+02:00">October 4, 1939—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>306. My 290, October 1, 5 p.m. Mr. Michel Moscicki has again called and states
                    that he found his father in very good shape. He said that the Rumanian Prime
                        Minister<anchor n="58" xml:id="d652-f1"/> had especially requested that no
                        <pb facs="0704" n="696" xml:id="pg696"/> mention be made of the
                    ex-President’s idea of possibly going eventually to the United States. He
                    confirmed my understanding that he would go first to Switzerland and said that
                    all arrangements had now been made. As you may know ex-President Moscicki
                    acquired Swiss nationality when there as a young man and has never renounced
                    it.</p>
                <p>Above not repeated to Paris.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
